                <pb facs="0299" n="227" xml:id="pg227"/>

                <head>No. 114.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to Mr.
                    Lowell</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-03-16"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 16,
                        1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>[The Secretary of State refers to his telegram of the 4th instant expressive of
                    the President’s wishes as to early trials, and states that the President, though
                    desirous of not seeming unreasonable, would be relieved if, without having to
                    wait for general action regarding coercion act cases, he could inform Congress
                    that his request regarding the Americans has been complied with.]</p>
            </div>
